1. PURPOSE
The API Gateway, Rate Limiting & Service Protection Architecture establishes the controlled request boundary through which external clients, frontend applications, integrations and internal services communicate with OneDraft.
The purpose of this subsystem is to provide centralized request admission, routing, authentication integration, authorization enforcement, rate limiting, quota enforcement, abuse protection, request validation, traffic control and service protection.
The API gateway shall provide a controlled boundary between request sources and the internal OneDraft service architecture.
2. ARCHITECTURAL ROLE
The API Gateway forms the primary request-control layer of OneDraft.
It connects external and internal request sources to the application, domain, runtime, AI, computational, artifact and administrative services.
Primary responsibility: Admit, validate, protect, route and observe requests.
Primary inputs: HTTP requests, API credentials, authenticated sessions, service credentials, tenant context and request metadata.
Primary outputs: Authorized service requests, rate-limit decisions, routing decisions, structured errors, telemetry and request records.
Authority: The gateway shall enforce request-level controls while downstream services remain authoritative for domain operations and business rules.
3. GATEWAY ARCHITECTURE
The canonical request path shall follow:
CLIENT
   ↓
API GATEWAY
   ↓
REQUEST VALIDATION
   ↓
AUTHENTICATION
   ↓
TENANT CONTEXT
   ↓
AUTHORIZATION
   ↓
RATE LIMITING
   ↓
ENTITLEMENT / QUOTA CHECK
   ↓
ROUTING
   ↓
APPLICATION SERVICE
   ↓
DOMAIN / COMPUTATIONAL SERVICE
The gateway shall not replace application authorization, domain validation or lifecycle controls.
4. REQUEST SOURCES
OneDraft shall support controlled request sources including:
Web Frontend
Mobile or Future Clients
Public API Clients
Organization Integrations
SDK Clients
Administrative Clients
Internal Services
Worker Services
Each request source shall use the authentication and authorization mechanism appropriate to its trust level.
5. API BOUNDARY
The gateway shall provide a stable external API boundary.
External clients shall not directly access internal service endpoints unless explicitly authorized through an internal service architecture.
Internal implementation details shall remain separated from public API contracts.
6. API VERSIONING
Public APIs shall be versioned.
A representative structure may include:
/api/v1/...
Major API versions shall define explicit compatibility boundaries.
Breaking changes shall require a new major API version or an equivalent controlled versioning mechanism.
7. REQUEST LIFECYCLE
Every API request shall pass through a defined request lifecycle.
RECEIVE
   ↓
PARSE
   ↓
VALIDATE
   ↓
AUTHENTICATE
   ↓
ESTABLISH TENANT CONTEXT
   ↓
AUTHORIZE
   ↓
RATE LIMIT
   ↓
CHECK ENTITLEMENT
   ↓
ROUTE
   ↓
EXECUTE
   ↓
RESPOND
   ↓
TELEMETRY
Failed requests shall terminate at the earliest appropriate control boundary.
8. REQUEST IDENTIFICATION
Each request shall receive a unique request identifier.
The identifier shall be propagated through applicable application services, workers, events and telemetry.
Request identifiers shall support:
Tracing
Debugging
Audit Correlation
Error Investigation
Operational Analysis
9. CORRELATION IDENTIFIERS
Requests participating in a larger workflow shall support correlation identifiers.
A correlation identifier may connect:
API Request
Aria Command
Job
Worker Execution
Event
Artifact Generation
Validation
Drawing Generation
This enables end-to-end operational tracing.
10. REQUEST VALIDATION
The gateway shall perform structural request validation before routing requests to application services.
Validation may include:
HTTP Method
Path
Headers
Content Type
Payload Size
Schema
Required Parameters
Encoding
Malformed requests shall be rejected before entering the application layer.
11. OPENAPI CONTRACT
Public API behavior shall be defined through the OneDraft OpenAPI contract.
The gateway shall enforce or support:
Endpoint Definitions
Request Schemas
Response Schemas
Authentication Schemes
Error Structures
Versioning
Content Types
The gateway contract shall remain synchronized with application implementation.
12. AUTHENTICATION INTEGRATION
The gateway shall integrate with the OneDraft Identity and Access architecture.
Authentication may use:
Session Authentication
OAuth/OIDC
API Keys
Service Credentials
Signed Tokens
The selected mechanism shall depend upon the request source.
Authentication establishes identity but does not independently establish authorization.
13. AUTHORIZATION INTEGRATION
Authorization shall be evaluated using the OneDraft authorization architecture.
The gateway shall establish sufficient context for downstream authorization.
Authorization shall consider:
User
Organization
Project
Role
Permission
Resource
Operation
The gateway shall not infer authorization solely from a valid authentication token.
14. TENANT CONTEXT
Every tenant-aware request shall establish organization context before reaching protected application services.
The gateway shall propagate:
Organization Identifier
User Identifier
Project Identifier, where available.
Membership Context
Authorization Context
The downstream service shall independently verify sensitive resource access.
15. SERVICE-TO-SERVICE AUTHENTICATION
Internal services shall authenticate to one another using controlled service identities.
Internal requests may contain:
Service Identity
Request Identity
Organization Context
Project Context
Correlation Identifier
Internal authentication shall prevent an external user from impersonating an internal service.
16. ROUTING
The gateway shall route requests according to explicit service boundaries.
Representative routes include:
/api/v1/projects
/api/v1/models
/api/v1/validation
/api/v1/compliance
/api/v1/drawings
/api/v1/reviews
/api/v1/aria
/api/v1/jobs
/api/v1/artifacts
/api/v1/billing
/api/v1/organizations
Routing shall remain independent from frontend implementation details.
17. SERVICE DISCOVERY
Internal routing may use service discovery mechanisms appropriate to the deployment environment.
The gateway shall not hard-code unstable infrastructure addresses where dynamic service discovery is available.
Service endpoints shall be configurable through the infrastructure and configuration architectures.
18. RATE LIMITING
Rate limiting shall control request frequency.
Rate limits may apply to:
User
Organization
API Key
IP Address
Endpoint
Service
Project
Subscription
Rate-limit configuration shall remain versioned and administratively controlled.
19. RATE-LIMITING ALGORITHM
The implementation may use mechanisms such as:
Token Bucket
Leaky Bucket
Sliding Window
Fixed Window
The selected mechanism shall support the required traffic profile and operational guarantees.
The exact implementation shall remain an infrastructure-level decision provided that externally observable behavior satisfies the defined API contract.
20. RATE-LIMIT KEYS
Rate-limit keys shall be scoped appropriately.
Examples include:
user:{user_id}
organization:{organization_id}
api_key:{key_id}
endpoint:{route}
Composite keys may be used where multiple dimensions must be enforced simultaneously.
Tenant-aware limits shall not use global identifiers that could cause one organization's traffic to affect another organization's quota unless explicitly intended.
21. BURST CONTROL
Rate limiting shall support controlled traffic bursts.
Burst capacity may permit short-term increases above the sustained rate while maintaining a defined average throughput.
Burst behavior shall be documented for API consumers.
22. CONCURRENCY LIMITS
Some operations shall require concurrency limits rather than request-frequency limits.
Examples include:
Geometry Computation
Large Model Regeneration
Drawing Generation
Rendering
Bulk Export
AI Processing
Concurrency limits shall protect workers and computational resources.
23. REQUEST SIZE LIMITS
The gateway shall enforce request-size limits.
Limits shall apply to:
JSON Payloads
Multipart Uploads
Imported CAD Files
BIM Files
Images
PDFs
Large files shall use controlled artifact-upload mechanisms rather than unrestricted API payloads where appropriate.
24. TIMEOUTS
Gateway and service timeouts shall be explicitly configured.
Timeout classes may include:
Standard API
Interactive API
AI Request
File Upload
File Download
Long-Running Job Submission
Long-running operations shall normally be represented by asynchronous jobs rather than extended synchronous requests.
25. LONG-RUNNING OPERATIONS
Operations such as geometry regeneration, large drawing packages and extensive compliance analysis shall use asynchronous execution where appropriate.
The API shall return a job reference.
POST /operation
      ↓
JOB CREATED
      ↓
202 ACCEPTED
      ↓
GET /jobs/{job_id}
The worker system shall perform the operation independently of the original HTTP request.
26. IDEMPOTENCY
Mutating API operations shall support idempotency where repeated requests could create duplicate effects.
Examples include:
Project Creation
Job Submission
Payment-Related Operations
Artifact Creation
Export Requests
An idempotency key shall identify logically identical requests.
27. RETRY SAFETY
The gateway and clients shall distinguish retry-safe operations from non-idempotent operations.
The gateway shall not blindly retry requests that could create duplicate domain effects.
Retry behavior shall be coordinated with application services and job execution.
28. ERROR HANDLING
The API shall return structured error responses.
A canonical error structure shall contain:
Error Code
Message
Request Identifier
Correlation Identifier
Details, where appropriate.
Errors shall distinguish:
Authentication Failure
Authorization Failure
Validation Failure
Rate Limit
Quota
Not Found
Conflict
Service Unavailable
Internal Failure
29. RATE-LIMIT RESPONSE
When a request is rate-limited, the API shall provide an appropriate structured response.
The response may include:
HTTP 429
Error Code
Retry Information
Request Identifier
Rate-limit responses shall not expose internal infrastructure details.
30. ENTITLEMENT INTEGRATION
The gateway shall integrate with the Billing, Subscription & Entitlement architecture.
Before protected commercial capabilities are invoked, the applicable entitlement may be evaluated.
Examples include:
AI Access
CAD Export
BIM Export
Advanced Validation
Large Computation
API Access
The gateway shall enforce coarse-grained commercial access while application services enforce domain-specific conditions.
31. QUOTA INTEGRATION
Quota checks shall be performed where request admission depends upon available organizational capacity.
The gateway may perform fast quota checks while authoritative usage accounting remains within the billing and usage architecture.
Quota exhaustion shall result in a deterministic response.
32. ABUSE PROTECTION
The gateway shall provide controls against abusive request patterns.
Controls may include:
Rate Limiting
Burst Control
Concurrency Limits
Payload Limits
Authentication Requirements
IP Reputation Controls
Request Pattern Detection
Automated Blocking
These controls shall be configurable and observable.
33. BOT AND AUTOMATION CONTROL
Public-facing endpoints may require additional controls against automated abuse.
Controls may include:
API Credentials
Session Validation
Challenge Mechanisms
Request Limits
Behavioral Controls
The platform shall avoid imposing unnecessary controls on legitimate automated integrations.
34. IP CONTROLS
Where required, OneDraft may support:
IP Allow Lists
IP Deny Lists
Organization Network Restrictions
Administrative Access Restrictions
IP controls shall supplement identity-based authorization rather than replace it.
35. CORS
Browser-based API access shall use explicit Cross-Origin Resource Sharing policies.
Allowed origins shall be controlled by environment and application configuration.
Wildcard origins shall not be used for authenticated administrative operations unless explicitly justified.
36. CSRF PROTECTION
Browser-based authenticated operations shall implement appropriate CSRF protection where session-based authentication is used.
Token-based API authentication shall use the applicable security model.
CSRF controls shall remain coordinated with the frontend authentication architecture.
37. HEADER SECURITY
The gateway shall establish appropriate security headers.
Headers may address:
Content Security
Frame Restrictions
Content Type Handling
Transport Security
Referrer Policy
Browser Feature Restrictions
The exact policy shall be established through the security architecture.
38. TLS
External API communication shall use encrypted transport.
Production endpoints shall require current secure TLS configuration.
Internal service communication shall use encrypted transport where deployment topology and threat model require it.
Certificates shall be managed through the infrastructure and secrets architecture.
39. WAF INTEGRATION
OneDraft may deploy a Web Application Firewall in front of the API gateway.
The WAF may provide:
Common Attack Detection
Request Filtering
Protocol Enforcement
Bot Protection
Traffic Controls
The WAF shall complement rather than replace application security controls.
40. REQUEST LOGGING
The gateway shall produce structured request telemetry.
Records may include:
Request Identifier
Timestamp
Method
Route
Status
Latency
Organization
User
Service
Rate-Limit Result
Sensitive request payloads shall not be logged by default.
41. SENSITIVE DATA PROTECTION
Gateway logging shall prevent unnecessary exposure of:
Passwords
Tokens
API Keys
Payment Credentials
Private Project Data
AI Context
Sensitive Files
Sensitive headers and payload fields shall be redacted or excluded.
42. OBSERVABILITY
The gateway shall integrate with OneDraft observability.
Metrics shall include:
Request Rate
Error Rate
Latency
Throughput
Rate-Limit Events
Authentication Failures
Authorization Failures
Timeouts
Upstream Failures
Gateway Availability
Distributed traces shall preserve request and correlation identifiers.
43. HEALTH MANAGEMENT
The gateway shall monitor downstream service health.
Health states may include:
HEALTHY
DEGRADED
UNAVAILABLE
Routing behavior may adapt according to service availability.
The gateway shall not route traffic to unavailable service instances where service discovery supports health-aware routing.
44. CIRCUIT BREAKING
The gateway or service mesh may use circuit breakers to prevent repeated requests to failing services.
Circuit states may include:
CLOSED
OPEN
HALF_OPEN
Circuit breaking shall reduce cascading failures.
45. LOAD SHEDDING
Where service capacity is constrained, the platform may reject lower-priority or excess traffic to preserve core system availability.
Load shedding shall be deterministic and observable.
Critical operations shall receive appropriate priority according to platform policy.
46. PRIORITY MANAGEMENT
Requests may receive priority classifications.
Potential priority levels include:
INTERACTIVE
STANDARD
BACKGROUND
ADMINISTRATIVE
SYSTEM_CRITICAL
Priority controls shall be applied carefully to prevent starvation of ordinary tenant workloads.
47. MULTI-TENANT PROTECTION
Rate limiting, concurrency and capacity controls shall respect tenant boundaries.
One organization's workload shall not unintentionally consume all shared capacity.
Organization-level limits shall integrate with subscription entitlements and platform capacity controls.
The gateway shall support fair resource allocation.
48. SECURITY, TESTING & ARCHITECTURAL INVARIANTS
The API Gateway shall be tested against:
Authentication Bypass
Authorization Bypass
Cross-Tenant Access
Rate-Limit Bypass
Quota Bypass
Payload Abuse
Request Smuggling
Malformed Requests
Replay Attempts
Idempotency Failures
Timeouts
Service Failures
Concurrent Requests
Load Conditions
The following invariants shall govern the subsystem.
Backend Authority: Gateway controls shall not replace domain authorization.
Default Denial: Protected endpoints shall require explicit authorization.
Tenant Isolation: Requests shall retain tenant context throughout execution.
Rate Enforcement: Defined limits shall be enforced server-side.
Idempotency: Retryable mutations shall not produce uncontrolled duplicate effects.
No Credential Logging: Sensitive credentials shall not be exposed through telemetry.
Long-Running Separation: Long-running workloads shall use asynchronous execution where appropriate.
Service Protection: Failure of one subsystem shall not automatically cascade across the platform.
Observability: Every request shall be traceable through a request or correlation identifier.
API Stability: Public contracts shall remain versioned and controlled.
49. IMPLEMENTATION REQUIREMENTS
The implementation shall provide:
API Gateway Service
Authentication Middleware
Tenant Context Middleware
Authorization Middleware
Rate-Limit Service
Quota Integration
Request Validation
Routing Layer
Error Handling
Request Telemetry
Security Middleware
Health Checking
Circuit Breaking
Service Discovery Integration
API Version Management
The gateway shall integrate with PostgreSQL, Redis, the event bus, worker system, identity system, billing system and observability platform according to the established OneDraft architecture.
50. COMPLETION STATUS
The API Gateway, Rate Limiting & Service Protection Architecture establishes the controlled request boundary for OneDraft.
The subsystem defines API versioning, request validation, authentication, authorization integration, tenant context, routing, rate limiting, concurrency limits, payload protection, timeouts, asynchronous operations, idempotency, error handling, entitlement and quota integration, abuse protection, CORS, CSRF, TLS, WAF integration, observability, health management, circuit breaking, load shedding, priority management and multi-tenant service protection.
The architecture provides the request-admission and protection layer required for OneDraft to operate reliably as a multi-tenant CAD-AI platform.
The next architectural layer establishes the Performance, Scalability & Capacity Architecture, defining how OneDraft accommodates geometry workloads, concurrent projects, queue scaling, database growth, computational demand and production capacity.
